​ ```
3. `TIFFCreateDirectory`
​ `TIFFCreateDirectory` initializes a new in-memory TIFF directory by resetting the current tags/state (`TIFFDefaultDirectory`) and clearing offsets/position fields. Use it when starting a fresh directory setup. It always returns `0`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern int TIFFCreateDirectory(TIFF*);
​ ```
4. `TIFFWriteDirectory`
​ `TIFFWriteDirectory` is a convenience API to write the current TIFF directory/IFD to the file. Use it after setting tags and image data on a `TIFF*`. It simply calls `TIFFWriteDirectorySec(tif, TRUE, TRUE, NULL)`, so behavior is delegated there; it returns that function’s status.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern int TIFFWriteDirectory(TIFF *);
​ ```
5. `TIFFFreeDirectory`
​ `TIFFFreeDirectory` releases memory owned by a TIFF directory in `tif->tif_dir`: it clears field-set bits, frees allocated tag arrays/custom tag values, and resets related state. Use it when discarding/replacing a directory to avoid leaks; it doesn’t close the TIFF file.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern void TIFFFreeDirectory(TIFF*);
​ ```
6. `TIFFWriteCheck`
​ `TIFFWriteCheck` validates a TIFF before writing. Use it before outputting data: it rejects read-only files, mismatched write mode (tiles vs scanlines), and missing required fields like `ImageWidth`. On failure it reports via `TIFFErrorExt` and returns 0; otherwise it prepares write state and returns success.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern int TIFFWriteCheck(TIFF *, int, const char *);
​ ```
7. `TIFFDeferStrileArrayWriting`
​ `TIFFDeferStrileArrayWriting()` enables deferred writing of strile arrays for a TIFF being written. Call it only on a writable TIFF before any directory is written. It returns 1 on success; 0 if opened read-only or if the directory was already written.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern int TIFFDeferStrileArrayWriting(TIFF *);
​ ```

