Task: Write a C fuzz driver program to fuzz the library libtiff using the information provided below. Reply with only the content of the C source file enclosed in triple backticks (```), without any additional explanation.

## Target API Functions

1. `TIFFGetReadProc`
​ `TIFFGetReadProc` returns the current read callback stored in a `TIFF*`. Use it to inspect which function libtiff will call for reads. It doesn’t read data itself; it just returns `tif->tif_readproc` (or NULL if unset).
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFFReadWriteProc TIFFGetReadProc(TIFF*);
​ ```
2. `TIFFGetMapFileProc`
​ `TIFFGetMapFileProc` returns the current file-mapping callback stored in a `TIFF*`. Use it to inspect which `TIFFMapFileProc` a TIFF handle will use for mapping. It does no work itself and simply returns `tif->tif_mapproc` (possibly `NULL` if unset).
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFFMapFileProc TIFFGetMapFileProc(TIFF*);
​ ```
3. `TIFFFdOpen`
​ `TIFFFdOpen` creates a `TIFF*` from an existing file descriptor on Win32, using libtiff client I/O callbacks. Use it with a valid `fd`, file `name`, and `mode`. On success it returns a TIFF handle and stores `fd`; on failure it returns `NULL`.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
/* __WIN32__ */
extern TIFF* TIFFFdOpen(int, const char*, const char*);
​ ```
4. `TIFFClientOpen`
​ `TIFFClientOpen` creates a TIFF handle using caller-supplied I/O callbacks, so libtiff can read/write from custom sources (memory, sockets, etc.). Pass `name/mode`, `clientdata`, and valid read/write/seek/close/size/map/unmap funcs. It returns `TIFF*` or fails if setup/config is invalid.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFF* TIFFClientOpen(const char*, const char*,
 thandle_t,
 TIFFReadWriteProc, TIFFReadWriteProc,
 TIFFSeekProc, TIFFCloseProc,
 TIFFSizeProc,
 TIFFMapFileProc, TIFFUnmapFileProc);
​ ```
5. `TIFFCleanup`
​ `TIFFCleanup` is the internal teardown for a `TIFF*`: it flushes pending writes unless opened read-only, calls the format-specific cleanup, frees directory/client-info data, and releases owned buffers/mapped file contents. Use it when closing/destroying a TIFF handle; don’t use after the handle is no longer valid.
Treat TIFFCleanup as consuming libtiff's internal field registry state. Ensure all custom tag metadata previously passed into libtiff remains valid for the entire TIFF handle lifetime; avoid stack-allocated or prematurely freed field_name pointers, and only destroy those buffers after the TIFF object is fully closed.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern void TIFFCleanup(TIFF* tif);
​ ```
6. `TIFFClientdata`
​ `TIFFClientdata(TIFF*)` returns the client-supplied I/O handle (`thandle_t`) stored in the `TIFF` object. Use it to retrieve the opaque stream/file context passed when the TIFF was opened. It just returns `tif->tif_clientdata`; no extra checks or side effects.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern thandle_t TIFFClientdata(TIFF*);
​ ```

