​ `TIFFGetWriteProc` returns the current write callback stored in a `TIFF*`. Use it to inspect which function libtiff will use for output I/O. It does not write data or modify state; it simply returns `tif->tif_writeproc` (or whatever is currently set).
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFFReadWriteProc TIFFGetWriteProc(TIFF*);
​ ```
3. `TIFFGetSeekProc`
​ `TIFFGetSeekProc` returns the current seek callback stored in a `TIFF*`. Use it to inspect the file’s seek handler, not to perform seeking. It simply returns `tif->tif_seekproc`; if unset, it returns whatever that field contains (possibly `NULL`).
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFFSeekProc TIFFGetSeekProc(TIFF*);
​ ```
4. `TIFFGetMapFileProc`
​ `TIFFGetMapFileProc` returns the current file-mapping callback stored in a `TIFF*`. Use it to inspect which `TIFFMapFileProc` a TIFF handle will use for mapping. It does no work itself and simply returns `tif->tif_mapproc` (possibly `NULL` if unset).
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFFMapFileProc TIFFGetMapFileProc(TIFF*);
​ ```
5. `TIFFClientOpen`
​ `TIFFClientOpen` creates a TIFF handle using caller-supplied I/O callbacks, so libtiff can read/write from custom sources (memory, sockets, etc.). Pass `name/mode`, `clientdata`, and valid read/write/seek/close/size/map/unmap funcs. It returns `TIFF*` or fails if setup/config is invalid.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFF* TIFFClientOpen(const char*, const char*,
 thandle_t,
 TIFFReadWriteProc, TIFFReadWriteProc,
 TIFFSeekProc, TIFFCloseProc,
 TIFFSizeProc,
 TIFFMapFileProc, TIFFUnmapFileProc);
​ ```
6. `TIFFGetCloseProc`
​ `TIFFGetCloseProc` returns the current close callback stored in a `TIFF*`. Use it to inspect which `TIFFCloseProc` will be used when the TIFF is closed. It just returns `tif->tif_closeproc`; no side effects or special cases.
​ Its signature is:
​ ```c
extern TIFFCloseProc TIFFGetCloseProc(TIFF*);
​ ```

